        'The most important classification is by dimension. "Dimension" here counts the FREE dimensions: the ones still larger than 100 nm that you could still shrink.',
        'Start with a bulk material: X, Y and Z all above 100 nm (3 free dimensions, a 3D/bulk material, not nano).',
        'Shrink one dimension to 1–100 nm: two free dimensions remain → a 2D nanomaterial: nano-plate, coating, layer, film, sheet (e.g. graphene).',
        'Shrink two dimensions: one free dimension remains → a 1D nanomaterial: nano-wire, rod, fiber, tube (hollow = nanotube).',
        'Shrink all three: zero free dimensions → a 0D nanomaterial = nanoparticle (also nanoclusters, quantum dots).',
        'A "3D nanomaterial" does not exist in this classification: if no dimension is below 100 nm it is a bulk material.',
        'Around 10 nm a second big shift happens: quantum materials (at least one dimension in 1–10 nm). Only quantum physics applies; an electron can be in two places at once.',
        'Quantum versions: one dimension < 10 nm → quantum well (film, coating); two → quantum wire; three → quantum dot, the smallest particle scientists have made.',
        'All quantum materials are nanomaterials, but not all nanomaterials are quantum materials (a 50 nm particle is nano, not quantum).',
        'Nano size in mathematics: anything below 1000 nm (1 µm) is "nano" as a prefix. In materials, only 1–100 nm counts, because that is where properties change.',
